// yours: scaffolded once, never touched again — forge will not overwrite this file
//
// The layout for every /auth route — and what it deliberately does NOT do.
//
// ── Why this file exists ──────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The root layout wraps the whole app in its chrome: the sidebar nav, the
// mobile nav, the header with the session control, the max-width content
// column. That is right for the product and wrong for every page under
// /auth, because a sign-in screen framed by the application's own
// navigation makes two false promises at once — that the visitor is inside
// the app, and that those nav links lead somewhere they can go. They are
// signed out. They cannot.
//
// It also looked broken in the obvious way: a sign-in card floating in the
// content column of a shell whose sidebar advertises a Dashboard the
// visitor has no session for.
//
// Next.js nests layouts by default, so a segment that wants LESS chrome
// than its parent has to say so. This file is that statement: it renders
// its children and nothing else, so the auth screens own the full viewport
// and paint their own background.
//
// ── What this means for the screens ───────────────────────────────────
//
// Each auth screen is responsible for its own full-page framing — they
// already use `min-h-screen` and set a background, which is exactly the
// shape a page gets when nothing above it contributes chrome.
//
// ── Yours to change ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// If your product WANTS branding on the sign-in page — a logo, a marketing
// panel beside the form, a footer with support links — this is where it
// goes. Everything added here applies to sign-in, the credential form and
// the OAuth callback alike, and to nothing else.

export default function AuthLayout({
  children,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode;
}) {
  return <>{children}</>;
}
